EXHIBIT A: CONSULTING ENGAGEMENT LETTER COMPLIANCE CHECKLIST

1.3 Engagement Letter Requirements

In accordance with the applicable standards and regulatory requirements, a consulting engagement letter must contain certain essential elements. The following are the minimum requirements that must be included in an engagement letter:

a. Scope of Services

(i) A clear description of the services to be provided by the consultant;

(ii) Any limitations on the scope of services or areas not covered by the agreement;

(iii) Any assumptions or conditions under which the consulting services will be rendered.

b. Professional Responsibilities and Obligations

(i) The consultant's responsibilities and obligations with respect to independence, confidentiality, and objectivity;

(ii) The consultant's duty to maintain adequate records and documents;

(iii) The consultant's obligation to comply with applicable laws, regulations, and standards.

c. Independence Requirements

(i) A statement confirming the consultant's independence from any party whose financial statements or business activities are being audited or reviewed;

(ii) A declaration of the consultant's lack of any material relationship with such parties that would impair their objectivity.

d. Confidentiality and Intellectual Property

(i) An undertaking by the consultant to maintain confidentiality of client information and trade secrets;

(ii) A statement regarding the ownership and control of intellectual property generated in connection with the engagement.

e. Dispute Resolution and Termination

(i) A provision outlining the process for resolving disputes arising out of or related to the engagement letter;

(ii) Conditions under which either party may terminate the agreement.

Accordingly, it is essential that any consulting engagement letter submitted for audit be reviewed for compliance with these minimum requirements.

**SETTLEMENT AGREEMENT ANALYSIS**

Pursuant to the Court's Order [Dkt. No. 123], the undersigned counsel has conducted an exhaustive review of the Settlement Agreement ("Agreement") executed between Plaintiff, XYZ Corporation, and Defendant, ABC Inc., on February 10, 2023 (the "Settlement Date").

The Agreement was entered into in connection with the pending litigation captioned XYZ Corporation v. ABC Inc., Case No. [Insert case number], wherein Plaintiff sought damages for alleged breaches of contract. The parties agreed to settle all claims arising from or related to the disputes outlined in the Amended Complaint, dated January 15, 2023.

The terms of the Agreement are as follows:

*   Defendant shall pay to Plaintiff a total sum of $250,000 (the "Settlement Amount");
*   Said payment is contingent upon Plaintiff's execution and delivery of the Release and Waiver ("Release") attached hereto;
*   The parties agree to dismiss with prejudice all claims arising from or related to the disputes outlined in the Amended Complaint.

Upon review, it appears that Defendant has fulfilled its obligations under the Agreement, having made timely payment of the Settlement Amount. However, Plaintiff's failure to execute and deliver the Release raises a red flag regarding potential non-compliance with the terms of the Agreement.

Further investigation is warranted to determine whether the parties' mutual consent was obtained prior to the execution of the Settlement Agreement. As such, I recommend that counsel for both parties be summoned to provide evidence of the existence of an agreement in writing and signed by all necessary parties.

**RISK FLAGGED:**

*   Potential breach of contract (Section 5 of the Agreement);
*   Unclear terms regarding mutual consent;
*   Incomplete execution of Release.
